module.exports = {
  title: 'State Management with Pinia',
  slug: 'state-management',
  order: 7,
  topics: [
    {
      title: 'Choosing a State Strategy',
      slug: 'state-management-options',
      order: 0,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>"How do you manage state in Vue?" is an architecture question wearing a library question's clothes. The weak answer names Pinia and stops. The strong answer starts from the observation that <em>most state should not be in a store at all</em>, walks through the options in order of increasing scope, and reaches for a store only when the state genuinely belongs to the whole application.</p>`,
        `<p>Getting this wrong is expensive in both directions. Put everything in a global store and you lose encapsulation, make components untestable in isolation, and end up debugging why a form on one page reset a value on another. Put nothing in a store and you prop-drill authentication state through nine layers.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Vue offers a ladder of options. Climb it only as far as the state's actual scope requires:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Scope of the state</th><th>Tool</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td>One component</td><td><code>ref</code> / <code>reactive</code> in setup</td><td>Is this dropdown open?</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Parent → child</td><td>props / emits</td><td>The item a row renders</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>A subtree</td><td><code>provide</code> / <code>inject</code></td><td>A <code>&lt;Tabs&gt;</code> sharing state with its <code>&lt;Tab&gt;</code> children; theme; form context</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Reused logic, isolated state</td><td>A composable</td><td><code>usePagination()</code>, <code>useFetch()</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>The whole application</td><td>Pinia</td><td>Authenticated user, cart, notifications</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>Server data</td><td>A query library</td><td>Cached API resources with refetching</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td>The URL</td><td>Vue Router</td><td>Current page, filters, pagination, search term</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `{{fig:ch8-state-ladder.svg|A ladder of state management options ordered by scope, from local refs through props, provide-inject and composables up to Pinia, with server state and URL state shown as separate categories|Climb only as far as the state's real scope requires — most state stops well below the top rung.}}`,
        `<p>The last two rows matter more than they look. <strong>Server state is not client state</strong>: it is a cache of something that lives elsewhere and can go stale, which is a different problem with different tools. And <strong>the URL is state</strong> — if a filter should survive a refresh or be shareable in a link, it belongs in the query string, not in a store.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Why a store at all?</strong> Two problems that the lower rungs cannot solve. First, <em>non-hierarchical sharing</em>: props and provide/inject flow downward, so two components in different branches — a header cart badge and a checkout page — have no path between them short of hoisting the state to their common ancestor, which is often the root. Second, <em>persistence across routes</em>: route components unmount on navigation, so state held in one disappears.</p>`,
        `<p>A store solves both by living outside the component tree entirely. Any component can import it; nothing unmounts it.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why not just a module with a <code>ref</code>?</strong> You can, and for a trivial case it works:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// store.js — a \"poor man's store\"\nimport { reactive } from 'vue'\nexport const state = reactive({ user: null })</code></pre>`,
        `<p>What you lose is worth naming, because interviewers ask exactly this: devtools integration (time-travel, state inspection), hot module replacement that preserves state, a convention for where mutations live, SSR safety, and plugin hooks for persistence. The last one is a genuine bug rather than a convenience — on a server, a module-scope <code>reactive</code> is created once per <em>process</em>, so one user's data leaks into another user's request. Pinia creates a fresh store instance per app instance, which is what makes it SSR-safe.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Server state deserves its own tool.</strong> Data fetched from an API has concerns client state does not: caching, deduplication of concurrent requests, background refetching, stale-while-revalidate, retry, and invalidation after a mutation. Hand-rolling that inside a Pinia store is a common and costly mistake — you end up reimplementing a query library badly. TanStack Query (Vue Query) or Nuxt's <code>useAsyncData</code> exist for this, and saying so signals real experience.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ① Local — the default. Don't promote it until you must.\nconst isOpen = ref(false)\n\n// ② Props/emits — parent to child\ndefineProps({ item: Object })\nconst emit = defineEmits(['select'])\n\n// ③ provide/inject — a subtree, e.g. a compound component\n// Tabs.vue\nprovide(tabsKey, { activeTab: readonly(activeTab), select })\n// Tab.vue (any depth below)\nconst { activeTab, select } = inject(tabsKey)\n\n// ④ Composable — reusable logic, ISOLATED state per call\nconst { page, next } = usePagination()   // each caller gets its own\n\n// ⑤ Pinia — one shared instance for the whole app\nconst auth = useAuthStore()              // every caller gets the SAME one\n\n// ⑥ URL — shareable, survives refresh, back button works\nrouter.push({ query: { ...route.query, status: 'active' } })</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The distinction that decides most cases — composable vs store:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// composables/useCounter.js — NEW state per call\nexport function useCounter() {\n  const count = ref(0)                    // inside the function\n  return { count, inc: () =&gt; count.value++ }\n}\n\nconst a = useCounter()\nconst b = useCounter()\na.count.value++\nconsole.log(b.count.value)   // 0 — independent\n\n// stores/counter.js — ONE shared instance\nexport const useCounterStore = defineStore('counter', () =&gt; {\n  const count = ref(0)\n  return { count, inc: () =&gt; count.value++ }\n})\n\nconst x = useCounterStore()\nconst y = useCounterStore()\nx.count++\nconsole.log(y.count)         // 1 — same store</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The module-scope singleton and why it is not a store:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ⚠️ Works in the browser, BREAKS on the server\nimport { reactive } from 'vue'\nexport const session = reactive({ user: null })   // one per Node process\n\n// Request A logs in as Ada → session.user = Ada\n// Request B (different user, same process) reads session.user → Ada\n// A cross-request data leak, and a security bug.\n\n// ✅ Pinia creates a fresh store per app instance, and SSR creates\n//    one app per request, so state can't cross requests.</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Server state — where a store is the wrong shape:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ❌ Reimplementing a cache badly inside a store\nexport const useProductStore = defineStore('products', () =&gt; {\n  const products = ref([])\n  const loading = ref(false)\n  const lastFetched = ref(0)\n\n  async function fetchAll() {\n    if (Date.now() - lastFetched.value &lt; 60_000) return   // hand-rolled staleness\n    loading.value = true\n    products.value = await api.getProducts()               // no dedup, no retry,\n    lastFetched.value = Date.now()                         // no background refresh\n    loading.value = false\n  }\n  return { products, loading, fetchAll }\n})\n\n// ✅ A query library owns caching, dedup, retry, invalidation and refetching\nimport { useQuery } from '@tanstack/vue-query'\n\nconst { data: products, isLoading } = useQuery({\n  queryKey: ['products'],\n  queryFn: api.getProducts,\n  staleTime: 60_000,\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting everything in the store.</strong> A dropdown's open state is not application state. Global state you did not need costs you encapsulation and testability.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Prop-drilling instead of injecting.</strong> Five layers of forwarding a value nobody in between uses is the signal to switch tools.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Confusing a composable with a store.</strong> Composables create fresh state per call; stores are shared. Choosing wrong gives you either accidental sharing or accidental isolation.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>A module-scope <code>reactive</code> as a store.</strong> No devtools, no HMR, and a genuine cross-request leak under SSR.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Caching server data in a store by hand.</strong> You will reimplement deduplication, staleness and invalidation, badly.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Storing derivable state.</strong> If it can be computed from other state, compute it. Two sources of truth drift.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Ignoring the URL.</strong> Filters and pagination in a store are lost on refresh and cannot be shared as a link.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>One giant store.</strong> Split by domain — auth, cart, notifications — so each has a clear owner and can be lazy-loaded.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Start local. Promote state only when a second consumer genuinely needs it.</li>`,
        `<li>Use provide/inject for subtree context — compound components, themes, form contexts — and Pinia for application state.</li>`,
        `<li>Put anything the user should be able to bookmark, share or restore with the back button in the URL.</li>`,
        `<li>Treat server data as a cache and use a query library for it; keep Pinia for client state.</li>`,
        `<li>Split stores by domain, one concern each.</li>`,
        `<li>Derive with <code>computed</code>/getters rather than storing a second copy.</li>`,
        `<li>Ask "who owns this?" before "where do I put it?" — the owner usually names the location.</li>`,
        `<li>Never rely on module-scope reactive state if the app might ever be server-rendered.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you manage state in a Vue app?"</strong><br>A: By scope, and I climb the ladder only as far as necessary. Component-local <code>ref</code> for anything one component owns; props and emits between parent and child; <code>provide</code>/<code>inject</code> for a subtree, like a compound component or a theme; composables for reusable logic with isolated state; and Pinia for state the whole application shares — auth, cart, notifications. Two categories sit outside that ladder: server data, which is a cache and belongs in a query library, and anything shareable or refresh-surviving, which belongs in the URL.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "When do you actually need a store?"</strong><br>A: When state is shared <em>non-hierarchically</em> or must outlive a route. Props and injection flow downward, so a header cart badge and a checkout page in different branches have no path between them without hoisting state to the root. And route components unmount on navigation, so anything held there disappears. A store lives outside the component tree, which solves both. If neither applies, local state or injection is simpler and more encapsulated.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Composable or store?"</strong><br>A: Whether the state should be shared. A composable declares its state inside the function, so every call site gets an independent instance — right for pagination, a form, a fetch. A store is a single instance every consumer sees — right for auth or a cart. You can force a composable to behave like a store by declaring the state at module scope, but then you lose devtools, HMR and SSR safety, so at that point use Pinia.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why not just export a <code>reactive</code> object from a module?" (trap)</strong><br>A: In a browser-only SPA it mostly works. Under SSR it's a real bug: module scope is per Node process, not per request, so the object is shared across every user hitting that server — one user's session data leaks into another's render. Pinia creates a fresh store instance per app instance and SSR creates one app per request, which is what makes it safe. You also lose devtools, time-travel, state-preserving HMR and plugin hooks like persistence.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Where does server data belong?"</strong><br>A: Not in a normal store, in most cases. Server data is a cache of something that lives elsewhere, so it has concerns client state doesn't — staleness, deduplicating concurrent requests, background refetching, retry, invalidation after a mutation. Hand-rolling that inside Pinia means reimplementing a query library. I'd use TanStack Query or, in Nuxt, <code>useAsyncData</code>, and keep Pinia for genuine client state like the current user or UI preferences.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Ladder: local <code>ref</code> → props/emits → provide/inject → composable → <strong>Pinia</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Store needed when state is shared <strong>non-hierarchically</strong> or must <strong>outlive a route</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Composable = <strong>new state per call</strong>. Store = <strong>one shared instance</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Module-scope <code>reactive</code> ⇒ no devtools/HMR + <strong>SSR cross-request leak</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Server state ≠ client state</strong> — use a query library (TanStack Query / <code>useAsyncData</code>).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>The URL is state</strong> — filters, sort, pagination, search go in the query string.</li>`,
        `<li>Derive with computed/getters; never store a second copy.</li>`,
        `<li>Split stores by domain; don't build one god store.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Pinia: Stores, State, Getters and Actions',
      slug: 'pinia-basics',
      order: 1,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Pinia is the official state library for Vue and the default answer in any modern Vue interview. Beyond the API, interviewers want to know <em>why it replaced Vuex</em> — the answer touches TypeScript, the removal of mutations, module nesting and bundle size, and it is a good proxy for whether you have followed the ecosystem or learned Vue from an old course.</p>`,
        `<p>The practical stakes: stores are the shared surface of an application. A store with unclear ownership and scattered mutations becomes the thing everyone is afraid to touch.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>A Pinia store has three parts, which map exactly onto concepts you already know:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Store part</th><th>Component equivalent</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>state</code></td><td><code>data</code> / <code>ref</code></td><td>The shared data</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>getters</code></td><td><code>computed</code></td><td>Derived, cached values</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>actions</code></td><td><code>methods</code></td><td>Functions that mutate state; may be async</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `<p>There are two syntaxes, and both produce identical stores:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Setup syntax — recommended; reads exactly like &lt;script setup&gt;\nexport const useCounterStore = defineStore('counter', () =&gt; {\n  const count = ref(0)                                  // state\n  const doubled = computed(() =&gt; count.value * 2)       // getter\n  function increment() { count.value++ }                // action\n  return { count, doubled, increment }                  // ← must return everything\n})\n\n// Options syntax — familiar if you come from Vuex\nexport const useCounterStore = defineStore('counter', {\n  state: () =&gt; ({ count: 0 }),\n  getters: { doubled: (state) =&gt; state.count * 2 },\n  actions: { increment() { this.count++ } },\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>The first argument is a unique <strong>id</strong> — Pinia uses it in devtools and for SSR state hydration, so it must be stable and unique.</p>`,
        `{{fig:ch8-pinia-store.svg|Anatomy of a Pinia store showing state as refs, getters as computeds and actions as functions, with components calling useStore to receive the same shared instance|A store is a component's script block, hoisted out of the tree: refs, computeds and functions, shared by everyone who asks for it.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><code>defineStore</code> returns a <em>function</em>, not a store. Calling that function resolves the active Pinia instance and returns the store, creating it on first call and returning the same instance thereafter. That lazy resolution is what makes stores SSR-safe: the instance belongs to the app, not to the module.</p>`,
        `<p>It also produces the one universal gotcha — <strong>you must call <code>useStore()</code> inside setup or after Pinia is installed.</strong> Calling it at module scope in a file imported before <code>app.use(createPinia())</code> throws "getActivePinia was called with no active Pinia."</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Destructuring breaks reactivity</strong>, for the same reason it does with any <code>reactive</code> object: the store is a reactive proxy, so pulling a primitive out copies it. <code>storeToRefs()</code> is the fix — it converts state and getters to refs while leaving actions alone (actions are plain functions and can be destructured freely).</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Why Pinia replaced Vuex</strong> — five concrete reasons, worth being able to list:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>No mutations.</strong> Vuex required a mutation for every synchronous change and an action for anything async, so a single field update needed a mutation, an action, a constant and a mapping. Pinia lets actions mutate state directly. Mutations existed for devtools time-travel, which Pinia achieves without them.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>TypeScript.</strong> Vuex's string-based <code>commit('setUser', payload)</code> and <code>dispatch</code> could not be typed properly. Pinia stores are plain objects with inferred types — autocomplete and compile-time checking work with no extra declarations.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No nested modules.</strong> Vuex modules brought namespacing, <code>rootState</code> and deeply nested access paths. Pinia has flat stores that import each other, which is simpler and tree-shakes.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Smaller and modular.</strong> Around 1.5 kB, and unused stores are tree-shaken out.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Composition API native.</strong> The setup syntax is just a <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code> block, so composables work inside stores.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<p><strong>Mutating state</strong> can be done directly (<code>store.count++</code>), in bulk (<code>store.$patch({ … })</code> or the function form for arrays), by replacing (<code>store.$state = { … }</code>), or reset (<code>store.$reset()</code> — options syntax only, since setup stores have no declarative initial state).</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// stores/cart.js — a realistic setup-syntax store\nimport { defineStore } from 'pinia'\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\nimport { useAuthStore } from './auth'\n\nexport const useCartStore = defineStore('cart', () =&gt; {\n  // ── state ──\n  const items = ref([])\n  const loading = ref(false)\n  const error = ref(null)\n\n  // ── getters ──\n  const count = computed(() =&gt; items.value.reduce((n, i) =&gt; n + i.qty, 0))\n  const subtotal = computed(() =&gt; items.value.reduce((s, i) =&gt; s + i.price * i.qty, 0))\n  const isEmpty = computed(() =&gt; items.value.length === 0)\n\n  // A getter that depends on ANOTHER store — just import and call it\n  const total = computed(() =&gt; {\n    const auth = useAuthStore()\n    const discount = auth.isPremium ? 0.1 : 0\n    return subtotal.value * (1 - discount)\n  })\n\n  // ── actions ── (may be async; mutate state directly)\n  function add(product, qty = 1) {\n    const existing = items.value.find(i =&gt; i.id === product.id)\n    if (existing) existing.qty += qty\n    else items.value.push({ ...product, qty })\n  }\n\n  function remove(id) {\n    items.value = items.value.filter(i =&gt; i.id !== id)\n  }\n\n  async function checkout() {\n    loading.value = true\n    error.value = null\n    try {\n      const order = await api.createOrder(items.value)\n      items.value = []\n      return order\n    } catch (e) {\n      error.value = e.message\n      throw e                 // let the component decide how to surface it\n    } finally {\n      loading.value = false\n    }\n  }\n\n  function $reset() {         // setup stores need this written by hand\n    items.value = []\n    error.value = null\n  }\n\n  return { items, loading, error, count, subtotal, isEmpty, total, add, remove, checkout, $reset }\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- Using it — note storeToRefs --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { storeToRefs } from 'pinia'\nimport { useCartStore } from '@/stores/cart'\n\nconst cart = useCartStore()\n\n// ❌ Destructuring the store copies primitives out of the proxy — dead\n// const { count, subtotal } = cart\n\n// ✅ storeToRefs keeps state and getters reactive\nconst { count, subtotal, isEmpty, loading } = storeToRefs(cart)\n\n// ✅ Actions are plain functions — destructure them directly\nconst { add, remove, checkout } = cart\n\n// Or skip destructuring entirely and use cart.count in the template\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;template&gt;\n  &lt;p v-if="isEmpty"&gt;Your cart is empty&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;p v-else&gt;{{ count }} items · ₹{{ subtotal }}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;button :disabled="loading || isEmpty" @click="checkout"&gt;Checkout&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/template&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Mutating state — the four ways:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">const cart = useCartStore()\n\n// ① Direct — perfectly idiomatic in Pinia, unlike Vuex\ncart.items.push(product)\n\n// ② $patch with an object — one devtools entry instead of three\ncart.$patch({ loading: false, error: null })\n\n// ③ $patch with a function — for arrays and complex changes\ncart.$patch((state) =&gt; {\n  state.items.push(product)\n  state.items.sort((a, b) =&gt; a.name.localeCompare(b.name))\n})\n\n// ④ Replace the whole state\ncart.$state = { items: [], loading: false, error: null }\n\n// Subscribe to changes (persistence, logging)\ncart.$subscribe((mutation, state) =&gt; {\n  localStorage.setItem('cart', JSON.stringify(state.items))\n})\n\n// Subscribe to actions (analytics, error reporting)\ncart.$onAction(({ name, args, after, onError }) =&gt; {\n  after((result) =&gt; analytics.track(\`cart.\${name}\`, { args, result }))\n  onError((err) =&gt; report(err, { action: name }))\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Setup and the module-scope trap:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// main.js — Pinia BEFORE the router, and both before mount()\nimport { createPinia } from 'pinia'\n\nconst app = createApp(App)\napp.use(createPinia())\napp.use(router)\napp.mount('#app')\n\n// ❌ router/guards.js — module scope runs at import time, before app.use()\nconst auth = useAuthStore()          // throws: no active Pinia\nrouter.beforeEach(() =&gt; auth.check())\n\n// ✅ Call it inside the guard, when Pinia is installed\nrouter.beforeEach(() =&gt; {\n  const auth = useAuthStore()\n  return auth.check()\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Destructuring the store.</strong> State and getters lose reactivity. Use <code>storeToRefs()</code>; actions are safe to destructure.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>useStore()</code> at module scope.</strong> Throws if the module is imported before <code>app.use(createPinia())</code>. Call it inside setup, a guard, or a function.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Installing Pinia after the router.</strong> Router guards that use stores then run against an uninstalled Pinia. Install Pinia first.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Expecting <code>$reset()</code> in a setup store.</strong> It only exists for the options syntax; write your own reset function.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting to return something from a setup store.</strong> Anything not returned is private — occasionally useful, usually a bug when a getter silently does not exist.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Building one giant store.</strong> Split by domain; stores can import each other freely.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Putting component-local state in a store.</strong> Shared state should be genuinely shared.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Swallowing errors in actions.</strong> Catch to set an error field, then rethrow so the caller can react.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Duplicate store ids.</strong> Two <code>defineStore('user', …)</code> calls collide in devtools and SSR hydration.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Prefer the setup syntax — it reads like a component, and composables work inside it.</li>`,
        `<li>Always use <code>storeToRefs()</code> when destructuring state or getters.</li>`,
        `<li>One store per domain: <code>auth</code>, <code>cart</code>, <code>notifications</code>. Cross-store access is just an import.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep components thin: they read getters and call actions, and never reimplement store logic.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>$patch</code> for multi-field updates so devtools shows one meaningful entry.</li>`,
        `<li>Handle loading and error state inside the action, and rethrow so the caller can respond.</li>`,
        `<li>Install Pinia before the router in <code>main.js</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Name the store function <code>useXStore</code> and the id <code>'x'</code>, consistently.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is Pinia and how is a store structured?"</strong><br>A: The official state library for Vue 3. A store has state (refs, or a <code>state</code> function), getters (computeds — derived and cached) and actions (functions that mutate state and may be async). Two syntaxes: setup, which is a function returning everything you want exposed and reads exactly like <code>&lt;script setup&gt;</code>; and options, which is closer to Vuex. <code>defineStore</code> returns a function you call inside setup to get the shared instance.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why did Pinia replace Vuex?"</strong><br>A: Five reasons. No mutations — actions mutate state directly, so a single field change no longer needs a mutation, an action, a constant and a mapping; mutations existed for devtools time-travel and Pinia gets that without them. Proper TypeScript, because stores are plain objects with inferred types rather than string-keyed <code>commit</code>/<code>dispatch</code>. No nested modules — flat stores that import each other. Much smaller, around 1.5 kB, and tree-shakeable. And it's Composition-API native, so composables work inside stores.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why does destructuring a store break reactivity?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Because the store is a reactive proxy, so <code>const { count } = store</code> reads the property once and copies the primitive into a new binding with no connection back. It's the same reason destructuring any <code>reactive</code> object fails. <code>storeToRefs(store)</code> converts state and getters into refs that proxy back to the store, so destructuring is safe. Actions are plain functions, not reactive state, so they can be destructured directly.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "I get 'getActivePinia was called with no active Pinia'. Why?"</strong><br>A: <code>useStore()</code> was called before Pinia was installed — almost always at module scope in a file imported during startup, like a router guards file. <code>defineStore</code> returns a function that resolves the <em>active</em> Pinia instance when called, and that instance doesn't exist until <code>app.use(createPinia())</code> runs. Move the call inside the guard or setup function. The same lazy resolution is what makes stores SSR-safe, since the instance belongs to the app rather than the module.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do stores communicate?"</strong><br>A: One imports and calls the other — <code>const auth = useAuthStore()</code> inside an action or getter of the cart store. There's no <code>rootState</code> or namespacing to navigate, because stores are flat and independent. Just call it inside the function rather than at module scope, so Pinia is guaranteed to be installed. Circular imports between two stores work at runtime for the same reason, since resolution is lazy.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What are <code>$patch</code>, <code>$subscribe</code> and <code>$onAction</code> for?"</strong><br>A: <code>$patch</code> applies several changes as one operation, so devtools shows a single meaningful entry instead of three — and the function form handles arrays and complex mutations. <code>$subscribe</code> fires on state changes, which is how persistence plugins write to localStorage. <code>$onAction</code> hooks action calls with <code>after</code> and <code>onError</code> callbacks — the natural place for analytics and error reporting without touching every action.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><code>defineStore(id, setupFn)</code> → returns a <strong>function</strong>; call it in setup to get the shared instance.</li>`,
        `<li>state = refs · getters = computeds · actions = functions (async OK, mutate directly).</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Setup stores must return everything</strong> they expose.</li>`,
        `<li><code>storeToRefs(store)</code> to destructure state/getters; actions destructure fine.</li>`,
        `<li>Never call <code>useStore()</code> at module scope — "no active Pinia".</li>`,
        `<li>Install Pinia <strong>before</strong> the router; both before <code>mount()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Cross-store: import and call the other <code>useXStore()</code> inside a function.</li>`,
        `<li><code>$patch</code> (object or fn) · <code>$state</code> · <code>$subscribe</code> · <code>$onAction</code> · <code>$reset</code> (options syntax only).</li>`,
        `<li>vs Vuex: no mutations · real TS · flat stores · ~1.5 kB · Composition-native.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Pinia in Practice: Plugins, Persistence, Testing and SSR',
      slug: 'pinia-advanced',
      order: 2,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>Knowing the store API is table stakes. What distinguishes a candidate with production experience is the surrounding work: persisting a cart across refreshes without breaking SSR, testing components that depend on stores without spinning up the whole app, and knowing why hydration mismatches happen.</p>`,
        `<p>These are also the areas where mistakes are expensive and invisible in development — a persistence plugin that writes an auth token to <code>localStorage</code>, or a test suite where stores leak state between cases and produce flaky failures.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>Four practical concerns sit on top of the basic store:</p>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Plugins</strong> — a function receiving every store as it is created, so cross-cutting behaviour (persistence, logging, injected services) is added in one place.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Persistence</strong> — writing state to <code>localStorage</code> or cookies and restoring it on load.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Testing</strong> — replacing real stores with controllable ones so component tests stay focused.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>SSR</strong> — serialising server-side state into the HTML and rehydrating it on the client.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `{{fig:ch8-pinia-ecosystem.svg|Pinia store surrounded by four concerns: a plugin layer wrapping store creation, persistence to localStorage, testing with createTestingPinia, and SSR state serialisation and hydration|The four things that surround a store in production: plugins, persistence, tests and SSR hydration.}}`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>Plugins.</strong> <code>pinia.use(fn)</code> registers a function called for every store, receiving <code>{ pinia, app, store, options }</code>. Whatever it returns is merged onto the store, so a plugin can add properties to every store at once. This is how <code>pinia-plugin-persistedstate</code> works: it reads the store's options, restores saved state on creation, and subscribes to changes.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Persistence</strong> has three decisions to get right. <em>What</em> to persist — never tokens or anything sensitive, since <code>localStorage</code> is readable by any script on the page and is therefore an XSS amplifier; auth tokens belong in an httpOnly cookie. <em>When</em> to write — <code>$subscribe</code> fires on every change, so debounce it for frequently updated state. <em>How to recover</em> — stored shapes drift as the app evolves, so validate on restore and discard anything that does not match, or a year-old <code>localStorage</code> entry will crash new code.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Testing.</strong> <code>createTestingPinia()</code> from <code>@pinia/testing</code> creates a Pinia whose actions are stubbed by default, so a component test can assert "the checkout action was called" without performing a network request. <code>initialState</code> seeds specific stores. Setting <code>stubActions: false</code> runs the real actions when you are testing integration. For unit-testing a store directly, <code>setActivePinia(createPinia())</code> in <code>beforeEach</code> gives each test a clean instance — without it, state leaks between tests and produces order-dependent failures.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>SSR.</strong> The server renders with a fresh Pinia per request, then serialises <code>pinia.state.value</code> into the HTML. The client creates its own Pinia and assigns that serialised state before mounting, so the first client render matches the server's markup exactly. Skip it and you get a hydration mismatch: the server rendered "3 items", the client starts empty and renders "0 items", and Vue warns and re-renders. Nuxt does all of this automatically — worth knowing, and worth knowing <em>what</em> it is doing.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// plugins/persist.js — a persistence plugin in ~25 lines\nexport function persistPlugin({ store, options }) {\n  // opt in per store: defineStore('cart', setup, { persist: { paths: ['items'] } })\n  if (!options.persist) return\n\n  const key = \`pinia:\${store.$id}\`\n  const paths = options.persist.paths ?? null\n\n  // ① Restore — validate, because the stored shape may be from an older version\n  try {\n    const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key) ?? 'null')\n    if (saved &amp;&amp; typeof saved === 'object') store.$patch(saved)\n  } catch {\n    localStorage.removeItem(key)      // corrupt or stale — drop it\n  }\n\n  // ② Persist on change, debounced\n  let timer\n  store.$subscribe((_mutation, state) =&gt; {\n    clearTimeout(timer)\n    timer = setTimeout(() =&gt; {\n      const data = paths\n        ? Object.fromEntries(paths.map(p =&gt; [p, state[p]]))\n        : state\n      localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(data))\n    }, 300)\n  }, { detached: true })              // survives the component that created the store\n}\n\n// main.js\nconst pinia = createPinia()\npinia.use(persistPlugin)\napp.use(pinia)</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// A plugin that adds something to EVERY store\npinia.use(({ store }) =&gt; {\n  store.$router = router          // available as this.$router in any action\n  store.$api = apiClient\n\n  // Log every action in development\n  if (import.meta.env.DEV) {\n    store.$onAction(({ name, args, after, onError }) =&gt; {\n      const start = performance.now()\n      after(() =&gt; console.log(\`✅ \${store.$id}.\${name}\`, \`\${(performance.now() - start).toFixed(1)}ms\`))\n      onError(err =&gt; console.error(\`❌ \${store.$id}.\${name}\`, err))\n    })\n  }\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Testing — components and stores:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Testing a COMPONENT that uses a store\nimport { mount } from '@vue/test-utils'\nimport { createTestingPinia } from '@pinia/testing'\nimport { vi } from 'vitest'\nimport CartSummary from '@/components/CartSummary.vue'\nimport { useCartStore } from '@/stores/cart'\n\nit('shows the item count and checks out', async () =&gt; {\n  const wrapper = mount(CartSummary, {\n    global: {\n      plugins: [\n        createTestingPinia({\n          createSpy: vi.fn,\n          initialState: {\n            cart: { items: [{ id: 1, price: 100, qty: 2 }] },   // keyed by store id\n          },\n          // stubActions defaults to true — actions are spies, not executed\n        }),\n      ],\n    },\n  })\n\n  expect(wrapper.text()).toContain('2 items')\n\n  const cart = useCartStore()          // the same testing store\n  await wrapper.find('button.checkout').trigger('click')\n  expect(cart.checkout).toHaveBeenCalled()   // no network request happened\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// Unit-testing a STORE directly\nimport { setActivePinia, createPinia } from 'pinia'\nimport { beforeEach, it, expect } from 'vitest'\nimport { useCartStore } from '@/stores/cart'\n\nbeforeEach(() =&gt; {\n  // ⚠️ Without this, stores are shared between tests and results depend on order\n  setActivePinia(createPinia())\n})\n\nit('merges quantities for an existing product', () =&gt; {\n  const cart = useCartStore()\n  cart.add({ id: 1, price: 100 }, 2)\n  cart.add({ id: 1, price: 100 }, 3)\n\n  expect(cart.items).toHaveLength(1)\n  expect(cart.count).toBe(5)\n  expect(cart.subtotal).toBe(500)\n})</code></pre>`,
        `<p>SSR — what Nuxt does for you:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// entry-server.js — a fresh Pinia per REQUEST (never module scope)\nexport async function render(url) {\n  const app = createApp()\n  const pinia = createPinia()\n  app.use(pinia)\n\n  await router.push(url)\n  await router.isReady()\n\n  const html = await renderToString(app)\n\n  // Serialise for the client. devalue handles Dates, Maps and undefined,\n  // which JSON.stringify silently mangles.\n  const state = devalue(pinia.state.value)\n\n  return { html, state }\n}\n\n// entry-client.js — rehydrate BEFORE mounting\nconst pinia = createPinia()\napp.use(pinia)\n\nif (window.__PINIA_STATE__) {\n  pinia.state.value = window.__PINIA_STATE__   // first client render now matches\n}\n\napp.mount('#app')\n\n// Skip this and you get a hydration mismatch: server said \"3 items\",\n// client starts empty and says \"0\", Vue warns and re-renders the subtree.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Persisting auth tokens to <code>localStorage</code>.</strong> Any injected script can read it. Use an httpOnly cookie for tokens; persist only non-sensitive UI state.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not validating restored state.</strong> A stored shape from an older release will crash new code. Validate and discard on mismatch, and consider a version key.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Un-debounced <code>$subscribe</code> persistence.</strong> Writing to <code>localStorage</code> on every keystroke is synchronous and blocks the main thread.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>No <code>setActivePinia</code> in tests.</strong> State leaks between tests and failures become order-dependent — the classic flaky suite.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting actions are stubbed by default in <code>createTestingPinia</code>.</strong> Asserting on a state change an action would have made fails, because the action never ran. Pass <code>stubActions: false</code> when that is the point of the test.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>A module-scope Pinia in SSR.</strong> One instance for all requests means one user's data reaches another's page. Create it per request.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Hydration mismatch from unserialised state.</strong> Assign the serialised state before <code>mount()</code>, not after.</li>`,
        `<li><strong><code>JSON.stringify</code> for SSR state.</strong> It loses <code>undefined</code>, <code>Date</code>, <code>Map</code> and <code>Set</code>. Use <code>devalue</code> or an equivalent.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Persisting on the server.</strong> <code>localStorage</code> does not exist there — guard plugins with a client check.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Persist deliberately: whitelist specific paths per store rather than persisting whole stores.</li>`,
        `<li>Keep tokens out of <code>localStorage</code>; httpOnly cookies for anything that grants access.</li>`,
        `<li>Version persisted state and discard on mismatch, so old entries cannot break a new release.</li>`,
        `<li>Debounce persistence writes and use <code>{ detached: true }</code> so the subscription outlives the creating component.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>createTestingPinia</code> for component tests and <code>setActivePinia(createPinia())</code> in <code>beforeEach</code> for store tests.</li>`,
        `<li>Seed only what the test needs via <code>initialState</code>, keyed by store id.</li>`,
        `<li>Create a fresh Pinia per request under SSR and rehydrate before mounting.</li>`,
        `<li>Use plugins for genuinely cross-cutting behaviour — persistence, logging, injected clients — not for business logic.</li>`,
        `<li>Reach for <code>pinia-plugin-persistedstate</code> rather than maintaining your own, unless you need something it does not do.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you persist Pinia state across reloads?"</strong><br>A: A plugin: on store creation, read the saved state and <code>$patch</code> it in, then <code>$subscribe</code> to write changes back — debounced, because <code>localStorage</code> is synchronous. <code>pinia-plugin-persistedstate</code> does this well. Three things matter: whitelist which paths persist rather than dumping whole stores; never persist tokens or anything sensitive, since <code>localStorage</code> is readable by any script and turns an XSS into a full account takeover; and validate on restore, because a stored shape from an older release will otherwise crash new code.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How do you test a component that depends on a store?"</strong><br>A: <code>createTestingPinia()</code> from <code>@pinia/testing</code> as a global plugin. It stubs actions by default, so I can assert an action was called without a real network request, and <code>initialState</code> seeds the store keyed by its id. If the test is about what an action actually does, I pass <code>stubActions: false</code>. For unit tests of the store itself, <code>setActivePinia(createPinia())</code> in <code>beforeEach</code> — without it, stores persist across tests and you get order-dependent flakiness.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What is a Pinia plugin?"</strong><br>A: A function registered with <code>pinia.use()</code> that runs for every store as it's created, receiving the store, its options, the app and the Pinia instance. Whatever it returns is merged onto the store, so you can add a property to every store at once — an API client, a router reference — or hook <code>$subscribe</code> and <code>$onAction</code> for persistence, logging and error reporting. It's the extension point for cross-cutting concerns, so those don't get copy-pasted into each store.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How does Pinia work with SSR?"</strong><br>A: The server creates a fresh Pinia per request — never at module scope, or one user's state leaks into another's render — renders, then serialises <code>pinia.state.value</code> into the HTML. The client creates its own Pinia, assigns that serialised state <em>before</em> mounting, and hydrates. Skipping the rehydration gives a mismatch: the server rendered "3 items", the client starts empty, Vue warns and re-renders. Use <code>devalue</code> rather than <code>JSON.stringify</code>, which loses <code>Date</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>Set</code> and <code>undefined</code>. Nuxt handles all of it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Your test asserts state changed after calling an action and it fails. Why?" (trap)</strong><br>A: <code>createTestingPinia</code> stubs actions by default — the action is replaced by a spy, so it records the call but never executes the body, and the state never changes. That's usually what you want in a component test, where you're asserting the component wired the click correctly. If you're testing the action's behaviour, either pass <code>stubActions: false</code> or unit-test the store directly with a real Pinia.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Plugin: <code>pinia.use(({ store, options, app }) =&gt; …)</code> — runs per store; returned object merges onto it.</li>`,
        `<li>Persistence = restore with <code>$patch</code> on create + <code>$subscribe</code> to write. Debounce; <code>{ detached: true }</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Never persist tokens</strong> to <code>localStorage</code> — httpOnly cookies. Whitelist paths. Version and validate.</li>`,
        `<li>Guard persistence plugins with a client check — no <code>localStorage</code> on the server.</li>`,
        `<li>Component tests: <code>createTestingPinia({ createSpy: vi.fn, initialState })</code>; <strong>actions stubbed by default</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Store tests: <code>setActivePinia(createPinia())</code> in <code>beforeEach</code> or tests leak state.</li>`,
        `<li>SSR: <strong>fresh Pinia per request</strong> → serialise <code>pinia.state.value</code> → assign on the client <strong>before mount</strong>.</li>`,
        `<li>Use <code>devalue</code>, not <code>JSON.stringify</code> (loses Date/Map/Set/undefined).</li>`,
        `<li><code>$subscribe</code> = state changes · <code>$onAction</code> = action calls with <code>after</code>/<code>onError</code>.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },

    {
      title: 'Migrating from Vuex to Pinia',
      slug: 'vuex-to-pinia',
      order: 3,
      content: [
        `<h2>Why it matters</h2>`,
        `<p>A large share of Vue jobs involve an existing Vuex codebase. Interviewers ask about migration to test two things at once: whether you know both libraries well enough to map concepts between them, and whether you approach a migration incrementally or propose a big-bang rewrite. The second is the real signal — "we'd run both side by side and migrate store by store" is a much better answer than "we'd rewrite it."</p>`,
        `<p>It is also a chance to demonstrate that you understand <em>why</em> the API changed, rather than treating Pinia as arbitrary churn.</p>`,
        `<h2>The concept, simply</h2>`,
        `<p>The mapping is mostly one-to-one, with one concept deleted:</p>`,
        `<table>`,
        `<thead><tr><th>Vuex</th><th>Pinia</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>`,
        `<tbody>`,
        `<tr><td><code>state</code></td><td><code>state</code> / refs</td><td>Same</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>getters</code></td><td><code>getters</code> / computeds</td><td>Same, no <code>rootState</code> argument</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>mutations</code></td><td>—</td><td><strong>Gone.</strong> Actions mutate directly</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>actions</code></td><td><code>actions</code></td><td>No <code>context</code>; use <code>this</code> or closures</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>modules</code></td><td>Separate stores</td><td>Flat; stores import each other</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>commit('setX', v)</code></td><td><code>store.x = v</code></td><td>Direct assignment</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>dispatch('fetch')</code></td><td><code>store.fetch()</code></td><td>A method call</td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>mapState</code>/<code>mapGetters</code></td><td><code>storeToRefs()</code></td><td>Or just <code>store.x</code></td></tr>`,
        `<tr><td><code>namespaced: true</code></td><td>—</td><td>Unnecessary — each store has its own id</td></tr>`,
        `</tbody>`,
        `</table>`,
        `{{fig:ch8-vuex-to-pinia.svg|Side-by-side mapping of a namespaced Vuex module with state, getters, mutations and actions onto a flat Pinia store, with the mutations layer crossed out and folded into actions|The migration is mostly mechanical: modules become flat stores and the mutations layer disappears into actions.}}`,
        `<p><strong>Why mutations went away</strong> is the question behind the question. In Vuex they existed so devtools could record every state change as a discrete, replayable event — that was their whole purpose. But they forced a rigid split: synchronous changes in mutations, async in actions, so every trivial field update meant writing a mutation, an action, a constant, and a mapping. Pinia achieves the same devtools traceability by patching the reactive state directly, so the ceremony buys nothing and was removed.</p>`,
        `<h2>How it works</h2>`,
        `<p><strong>They can coexist.</strong> Vuex and Pinia are separate plugins; installing both is fine. That is what makes an incremental migration possible: leave the Vuex store running, add Pinia, migrate one module at a time, and delete Vuex when the last module is gone. No feature freeze, no big-bang release.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>A recommended order:</strong></p>`,
        `<ol>`,
        `<li>Install Pinia alongside Vuex, both registered on the app.</li>`,
        `<li>Migrate leaf modules first — ones nothing else depends on.</li>`,
        `<li>For a module that must read Vuex state during the transition, have the Pinia store read from the Vuex store; the dependency is temporary and points in one direction.</li>`,
        `<li>Update consuming components as each store moves.</li>`,
        `<li>Migrate modules with dependents last, once their dependencies are already Pinia.</li>`,
        `<li>Remove Vuex.</li>`,
        `</ol>`,
        `<p><strong>Namespacing disappears</strong>, which is the biggest structural simplification. <code>store.dispatch('cart/items/add', payload)</code> becomes <code>useCartStore().add(payload)</code>. There is no <code>rootState</code>, no <code>rootGetters</code>, no <code>{ root: true }</code> — a store that needs another store imports it.</p>`,
        `<p><strong>Components change shape too.</strong> <code>mapState</code> and <code>mapGetters</code> become <code>storeToRefs()</code>, and <code>mapActions</code> becomes plain destructuring, since actions are ordinary functions.</p>`,
        `<h2>Code examples</h2>`,
        `<p>The same module, before and after:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── BEFORE: store/modules/cart.js (Vuex) ──\nexport default {\n  namespaced: true,\n\n  state: () =&gt; ({ items: [], loading: false }),\n\n  getters: {\n    count: (state) =&gt; state.items.reduce((n, i) =&gt; n + i.qty, 0),\n    subtotal: (state) =&gt; state.items.reduce((s, i) =&gt; s + i.price * i.qty, 0),\n    // reaching into another module\n    total: (state, getters, rootState, rootGetters) =&gt;\n      getters.subtotal * (rootGetters['auth/isPremium'] ? 0.9 : 1),\n  },\n\n  mutations: {\n    ADD_ITEM(state, item) { state.items.push(item) },\n    SET_LOADING(state, v) { state.loading = v },\n    CLEAR(state) { state.items = [] },\n  },\n\n  actions: {\n    async checkout({ commit, state, dispatch, rootState }) {\n      commit('SET_LOADING', true)\n      try {\n        await api.createOrder(state.items)\n        commit('CLEAR')\n        dispatch('notifications/push', { text: 'Ordered' }, { root: true })\n      } finally {\n        commit('SET_LOADING', false)\n      }\n    },\n  },\n}\n\n// Usage: store.dispatch('cart/checkout')  ·  store.getters['cart/count']</code></pre>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// ── AFTER: stores/cart.js (Pinia) ──\nimport { defineStore } from 'pinia'\nimport { ref, computed } from 'vue'\nimport { useAuthStore } from './auth'\nimport { useNotificationStore } from './notifications'\n\nexport const useCartStore = defineStore('cart', () =&gt; {\n  const items = ref([])\n  const loading = ref(false)\n\n  const count = computed(() =&gt; items.value.reduce((n, i) =&gt; n + i.qty, 0))\n  const subtotal = computed(() =&gt; items.value.reduce((s, i) =&gt; s + i.price * i.qty, 0))\n\n  // No rootGetters — just call the other store\n  const total = computed(() =&gt; {\n    const auth = useAuthStore()\n    return subtotal.value * (auth.isPremium ? 0.9 : 1)\n  })\n\n  // Mutations folded into the action; state mutated directly\n  async function checkout() {\n    loading.value = true\n    try {\n      await api.createOrder(items.value)\n      items.value = []\n      useNotificationStore().push({ text: 'Ordered' })   // no { root: true }\n    } finally {\n      loading.value = false\n    }\n  }\n\n  function add(item) { items.value.push(item) }\n\n  return { items, loading, count, subtotal, total, add, checkout }\n})\n\n// Usage: const cart = useCartStore(); cart.checkout(); cart.count</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Component code, before and after:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- BEFORE --&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\nimport { mapState, mapGetters, mapActions } from 'vuex'\n\nexport default {\n  computed: {\n    ...mapState('cart', ['items', 'loading']),\n    ...mapGetters('cart', ['count', 'subtotal']),\n  },\n  methods: {\n    ...mapActions('cart', ['checkout']),\n  },\n}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- AFTER --&gt;\n&lt;script setup&gt;\nimport { storeToRefs } from 'pinia'\nimport { useCartStore } from '@/stores/cart'\n\nconst cart = useCartStore()\nconst { items, loading, count, subtotal } = storeToRefs(cart)  // state + getters\nconst { checkout } = cart                                      // actions\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>`,
        `<p>Running both during the transition:</p>`,
        `<pre><code class="language-javascript">// main.js — both plugins, no conflict\nimport { createStore } from 'vuex'\nimport { createPinia } from 'pinia'\n\nconst app = createApp(App)\napp.use(vuexStore)        // legacy modules, still working\napp.use(createPinia())    // migrated stores\napp.mount('#app')\n\n// A Pinia store temporarily reading Vuex state — a one-way, temporary bridge\nexport const useCartStore = defineStore('cart', () =&gt; {\n  const items = ref([])\n\n  // auth hasn't been migrated yet\n  const isPremium = computed(() =&gt; vuexStore.getters['auth/isPremium'])\n\n  return { items, isPremium }\n})\n// Delete this line when auth becomes a Pinia store.</code></pre>`,
        `<h2>Common mistakes &amp; gotchas</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li><strong>Proposing a big-bang rewrite.</strong> They coexist; migrate module by module and ship continuously.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Recreating mutations in Pinia.</strong> Writing <code>setItems()</code> actions that only assign is porting the ceremony you were escaping.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Keeping the module hierarchy.</strong> Nested Pinia stores are not a thing; flatten into one store per domain.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Destructuring the store.</strong> The most common post-migration bug, because <code>mapState</code> did not have this problem. Use <code>storeToRefs</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Calling <code>useStore()</code> at module scope</strong> in a file that Vuex-era code imported early.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Looking for <code>$reset()</code> in a setup store.</strong> Options-syntax only; write one by hand.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Forgetting store ids must be unique</strong> and stable — they key devtools and SSR hydration.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Migrating the most-depended-on module first.</strong> Leaves the largest temporary bridge; do leaves first.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Not updating tests alongside.</strong> Vuex test helpers do not apply; each migrated store needs its Pinia-shaped tests in the same PR.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>Best practices</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Run both libraries and migrate incrementally, one store per pull request.</li>`,
        `<li>Start with leaf modules and work toward the most depended-upon.</li>`,
        `<li>Fold mutations into actions rather than porting them; that simplification is the point.</li>`,
        `<li>Flatten namespaced modules into one store per domain, named <code>useXStore</code>.</li>`,
        `<li>Convert cross-module access to plain imports, called inside functions.</li>`,
        `<li>Replace <code>mapState</code>/<code>mapGetters</code> with <code>storeToRefs</code> and <code>mapActions</code> with destructuring.</li>`,
        `<li>Migrate a store's tests in the same change as the store.</li>`,
        `<li>Delete Vuex only when the last module is gone, then remove the dependency.</li>`,
        `<li>Take the opportunity to add TypeScript types — inference is the main reason to be doing this at all.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
        `<h2>⭐ Interview lens</h2>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "How would you migrate a large Vuex codebase to Pinia?"</strong><br>A: Incrementally — they coexist as separate plugins, so there's no need for a big-bang rewrite or a feature freeze. Install Pinia alongside Vuex, migrate leaf modules first, and where a migrated store still needs unmigrated state, let it read from the Vuex store as a temporary one-way bridge. Update consumers as each store moves, migrate the most depended-upon modules last, and remove Vuex when the last one is gone. One store per PR, tests migrated in the same change.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Why did Pinia drop mutations?"</strong><br>A: They existed so devtools could record every state change as a discrete, replayable event. The cost was a rigid split — synchronous changes in mutations, async in actions — so updating one field meant a mutation, an action, a constant and a mapping. Pinia gets the same devtools traceability by tracking the reactive state directly, so the ceremony bought nothing. Actions now mutate state directly and can be sync or async.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What happens to namespaced modules?"</strong><br>A: They become flat, independent stores, one per domain. Namespacing existed to avoid name collisions in a single global tree; each Pinia store has its own id, so there's nothing to collide. <code>store.dispatch('cart/items/add', payload)</code> becomes <code>useCartStore().add(payload)</code>, and <code>rootState</code>, <code>rootGetters</code> and <code>{ root: true }</code> disappear entirely — a store that needs another one imports and calls it.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "What's the most common bug right after a migration?" (trap)</strong><br>A: Destructuring the store. <code>const { count } = useCartStore()</code> looks like the <code>mapState</code> it replaced, but the store is a reactive proxy, so it copies the primitive out and the value never updates — with no error. <code>storeToRefs()</code> is the fix for state and getters; actions are plain functions and destructure fine. The other frequent one is calling <code>useStore()</code> at module scope, which throws because Pinia isn't installed yet.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<blockquote><p><strong>Q: "Is there anything Vuex does that Pinia doesn't?"</strong><br>A: Not much that matters. Strict mode's enforcement of "state may only change in mutations" has no equivalent, because direct mutation is the model now — though <code>$subscribe</code> and <code>$onAction</code> give you the auditability that was the real goal. Vuex's module registration API and the mutation-based time-travel semantics also go, though Pinia's devtools cover time-travel. In practice the migration is a simplification, not a trade-off.</p></blockquote>`,
        `<h2>Cheat-card</h2>`,
        `<ul>`,
        `<li>Mapping: state→state · getters→getters · <strong>mutations→gone</strong> · actions→actions · modules→flat stores.</li>`,
        `<li><code>commit('SET_X', v)</code> → <code>store.x = v</code>. <code>dispatch('fetch')</code> → <code>store.fetch()</code>.</li>`,
        `<li><code>mapState</code>/<code>mapGetters</code> → <code>storeToRefs()</code>; <code>mapActions</code> → destructure.</li>`,
        `<li>No <code>namespaced</code>, no <code>rootState</code>/<code>rootGetters</code>, no <code>{ root: true }</code> — import the other store.</li>`,
        `<li>Mutations existed for devtools replay; Pinia gets it without them.</li>`,
        `<li><strong>Both can run at once</strong> ⇒ migrate one store per PR, leaves first.</li>`,
        `<li>Don't port mutations as <code>setX()</code> actions.</li>`,
        `<li>Post-migration bug #1: destructuring the store (use <code>storeToRefs</code>). #2: <code>useStore()</code> at module scope.</li>`,
        `<li>Setup stores have no <code>$reset()</code> — write one.</li>`,
        `</ul>`,
      ].join('\n'),
    },
  ],
}
